        "rapidmlx/qwen3.6:35b-a3b",
        "--title",
        "comparison launch",
        "A tar entry's size comes from the 12-byte size field of its header block (octal, or base-256 for large and negative numbers), from a pax extended header's 'size' record, or from a global extended header. Nothing stops any of them from being negative, and a negative size makes the reader miscount the bytes that follow the header. Refuse negative sizes in src/header.ts and src/pax.ts. In the Header class: when a block is decoded, the size it computes (the extended header's size, else the global extended header's size, else the block's own size field) becomes undefined if it is negative; and header data whose size is negative when compared as a number, whether passed to the constructor (new Header({ size: -1000 })) or merged in from an extended header, is ignored, so header.size stays undefined. In Pax.parse, a 'size' record is kept only when its numeric value is zero or greater: 'size=-1000' and a non-numeric value leave size undefined, while 'size=1000' gives 1000 and 'size=0' gives 0. Every other field (uid, gid, mode, the times and so on) keeps its current parsing, negative values included, and nothing else changes. Change only src/header.ts and src/pax.ts; do not edit tests. The tests import the built dist, so build with npm run prepare (tshy and an esbuild bundle) before running them; npm test runs prepare and then tap. The suite's assertions must keep passing: npm test -- --allow-incomplete-coverage (tap's 100% coverage rule already fails at the current commit on one branch of src/pax.ts, so it is not a rule here).\n\nVisible acceptance check (it must pass; it is not the only criterion): npm run prepare && node acceptance/visible/check.mjs\nThe project's own tests run with: npm test -- --allow-incomplete-coverage\nDo not modify anything under acceptance/visible/."
      ],
